Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["WS-1 and WS-2: the separation in the two tetrads", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"e67449ec-d221-4b29-9e72-e3cbe6c988d6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "These checks prove that the separation is exact. The spinor is written with \
the angular functions ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", 
    StyleBox["LG",
     FontSlant->"Plain"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSize->1.1 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0eff0f0c-1048-425f-a9c7-b7f957946c07"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Beta]", 
    StyleBox["LG",
     FontSlant->"Plain"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSize->1.1 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "73077c36-e26d-4870-9512-638f2b71cc2b"],
 " and two radial functions, it is put into the Dirac equation with the spin \
connection computed from the tetrad, and the angular and radial equations are \
used to eliminate the derivatives; the result must vanish identically. WS-1 \
does this in the static tetrad (outside the horizon), WS-2 in the freely \
falling tetrad, where the identity holds for every ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["r", "TI"], ">", "0"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSize->1.1 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3c961f2a-4955-4236-b5c6-53ffade84d54"],
 ": on both sides of the horizon, which is what Part IV needs to follow the \
field into the black hole."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a67caeac-4629-4425-969f-ae3ac7834b99"],

